public import Mathlib.Analysis.ODE.Gronwall

/-!
# Uniqueness of the particle flow

`FastDynamo/Dynamics/ParticleFlow.lean` states `IsParticleFlow v Φ` — `Φ_0 = id` together with
the ODE `d/dt Φ_t(x) = v(Φ_t(x))` read along a periodic lift — and
`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Flow/ParticleFlowProof.lean` proves it for the closed-form flow
`literalFlow`.  The paper speaks of **the unique** particle flow generated by `u`, and infers
existence *and* uniqueness from the global Lipschitz regularity of the velocity (the opening
paragraph of §4, citing `lem:global-velocity`).  This module supplies the uniqueness half, the
part of that sentence the closed formula does not give: for a Lipschitz velocity on the literal
three-torus there is at most one particle flow.

## The argument

Uniqueness is Grönwall's inequality applied **on the universal cover**, in line with the paper's
"the periodic lift of `u` and the corresponding equivariant lift of `Φ_t`" (proof of
`cor:ideal-growth`).  `Torus 3` is not a normed space, so no ODE uniqueness theorem applies to it
directly; but `IsParticleFlow.ode` hands out, for each `x : T³`, a curve `γ : ℝ → ℝ³` with
`Φ_t(x) = π(γ t)` and `γ'(t) = v(π(γ t))`, that is a solution of the *autonomous* ODE on `ℝ³` whose
field is the lifted velocity

```
  vNorm := v ∘ π : ℝ³ → ℝ³ ,
```

and `vNorm` is Lipschitz with the same constant as `v` because the quotient `π = literalTorusPoint`
is `1`-Lipschitz (`lipschitzWith_literalTorusPoint`; the sup metric of `T³ = Fin 3 →
UnitAddCircle` is dominated coordinatewise by the Euclidean metric of `ℝ³`).

Two flows `Φ, Φ'` give two such curves `γ, γ'` through the same torus point, so their starting
points agree *modulo the lattice*: `π(γ 0) = x = π(γ' 0)`.  The constant `m := γ 0 − γ' 0`
therefore satisfies `π m = 0`, and the translate `γ' + m` is again a solution of the same
autonomous ODE — the field is `ℤ³`-periodic — now with the *same* initial value `γ 0`.  Grönwall
(`dist_le_of_trajectories_ODE` of `Mathlib/Analysis/ODE/Gronwall.lean`, with `δ = 0`) forces
`γ = γ' + m` on every `Icc 0 b`, and the time-reversed system `r' = −vNorm(r)` gives the same on
`Icc 0 b` for the reflected curves, i.e. on every `Icc (−b) 0`.  Hence
`Φ_t(x) = π(γ t) = π(γ' t + m) = π(γ' t) = Φ'_t(x)` for all `t` and all `x`.

No existence theorem is used: existence is the closed formula of
`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Flow/TorusFlow.lean`, and Picard–Lindelöf is nowhere invoked.

## Contents

* `eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith` — the ODE uniqueness statement in the form this module
  needs: two everywhere-differentiable solutions of an autonomous Lipschitz ODE on a real normed
  space that agree at time `0` are equal, on the **whole** real line.
* `lipschitzWith_literalTorusPoint` — the quotient `ℝ³ → T³` is `1`-Lipschitz.
* `IsParticleFlow.unique` — the uniqueness theorem: a Lipschitz velocity on `T³` has at most one
  particle flow.
* `ScaleFamily.TorusCellPlacement.literalFlow_unique`, `existsUnique_isParticleFlow_literalVelocity`
  — at the constructed velocity of any admissible parameter tuple with `0 ≤ tau`, `literalFlow` is
  *the* particle flow.
* `literalFlow_mainParameterChoice_unique`, `existsUnique_isParticleFlow_mainLiteralVelocity`
  — the same at the parameters of `mainParameterChoice`, i.e. at the velocity
  `mainLiteralVelocity` of
  Theorem 1.2 (`thm:main`).  This is the paper's "the unique particle flow `Φ_t`" (opening
  sentence of §4), formalised; `FastDynamo/Dynamics/MainLagrangianClauses.lean` restates it as
  `mainLiteralFlow_unique`, at the name `mainLiteralFlow` the theorem's witness carries.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace FastDynamo

noncomputable section

open Metric Set
open scoped NNReal Topology

/-! ## Grönwall uniqueness for an autonomous Lipschitz field -/

/-- **Grönwall uniqueness at non-negative times.**  Two curves that solve the same autonomous ODE
`r' = w(r)` with a globally Lipschitz field `w`, differentiably at every real time, and that agree
at time `0`, agree at every `t ≥ 0`.

This is Mathlib's `dist_le_of_trajectories_ODE` on `Icc 0 (t + 1)` with `δ = 0`: the Grönwall
bound `δ · exp (K (t − a))` collapses to `0`, so the two curves are at distance `0`. -/
private theorem eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith_of_nonneg {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E]
    [NormedSpace ℝ E] {K : ℝ≥0} {w : E → E} (hw : LipschitzWith K w) {f g : ℝ → E}
    (hf : ∀ t : ℝ, HasDerivAt f (w (f t)) t) (hg : ∀ t : ℝ, HasDerivAt g (w (g t)) t)
    (h0 : f 0 = g 0) {t : ℝ} (ht : 0 ≤ t) : f t = g t := by
  have hfd : Differentiable ℝ f := fun s ↦ (hf s).differentiableAt
  have hgd : Differentiable ℝ g := fun s ↦ (hg s).differentiableAt
  have hfc : Continuous f := hfd.continuous
  have hgc : Continuous g := hgd.continuous
  have key := dist_le_of_trajectories_ODE (v := fun _ : ℝ ↦ w) (K := K) (f := f) (g := g)
    (a := 0) (b := t + 1) (δ := 0) (fun _ ↦ hw) hfc.continuousOn
    (fun s _ ↦ (hf s).hasDerivWithinAt) hgc.continuousOn (fun s _ ↦ (hg s).hasDerivWithinAt)
    (by simp [h0])
  have hdist : dist (f t) (g t) ≤ 0 := by
    simpa using key t ⟨ht, by linarith⟩
  exact dist_le_zero.1 hdist

/-- **Grönwall uniqueness on the whole line.**  Two curves that solve the same autonomous ODE
`r' = w(r)` with a globally Lipschitz field `w`, differentiably at every real time, and that agree
at time `0`, are equal.

The backward half is the forward half applied to the reflected curves `s ↦ f (−s)`, `s ↦ g (−s)`,
which solve the time-reversed system `r' = −w(r)` — a field with the same Lipschitz constant. -/
theorem eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    {K : ℝ≥0} {w : E → E} (hw : LipschitzWith K w) {f g : ℝ → E}
    (hf : ∀ t : ℝ, HasDerivAt f (w (f t)) t) (hg : ∀ t : ℝ, HasDerivAt g (w (g t)) t)
    (h0 : f 0 = g 0) : f = g := by
  have hwneg : LipschitzWith K fun y ↦ -w y := by
    refine LipschitzWith.of_dist_le_mul fun a b ↦ ?_
    simpa [dist_neg_neg] using hw.dist_le_mul a b
  have hreflect : ∀ u : ℝ → E, (∀ s : ℝ, HasDerivAt u (w (u s)) s) →
      ∀ s : ℝ, HasDerivAt (fun r : ℝ ↦ u (-r)) ((fun y ↦ -w y) ((fun r : ℝ ↦ u (-r)) s)) s := by
    intro u hu s
    have hcomp := (hu (-s)).scomp s (hasDerivAt_neg s)
    simpa [Function.comp_def] using hcomp
  funext t
  rcases le_total 0 t with ht | ht
  · exact eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith_of_nonneg hw hf hg h0 ht
  · have hback := eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith_of_nonneg (K := K) (w := fun y ↦ -w y)
      hwneg (hreflect f hf) (hreflect g hg) (by simpa using h0) (t := -t) (by linarith)
    simpa using hback

/-! ## The quotient `ℝ³ → T³` is `1`-Lipschitz -/

/-- **The quotient `π : ℝ³ → T³` is `1`-Lipschitz.**  The metric of `Torus 3 = Fin 3 →
UnitAddCircle` is the sup metric, the coercion `ℝ → UnitAddCircle` is `1`-Lipschitz
(`lipschitzWith_unitAddCircle_coe`), and a coordinate of a vector of `R3 = EuclideanSpace ℝ
(Fin 3)` is bounded by its Euclidean norm (`PiLp.norm_apply_le`).

This is the three-dimensional companion of `lipschitzWith_transverseTorusPoint`
(`FastDynamo/Dynamics/Flow/TorusFlow.lean`), whose source carries the sup metric of `ℝ × ℝ`
instead. -/
theorem lipschitzWith_literalTorusPoint : LipschitzWith 1 literalTorusPoint := by
  refine LipschitzWith.of_dist_le_mul fun x y ↦ ?_
  rw [NNReal.coe_one, one_mul]
  refine (dist_pi_le_iff dist_nonneg).2 fun j ↦ ?_
  have hcoord : dist (x j) (y j) ≤ dist x y := by
    rw [dist_eq_norm, dist_eq_norm, ← PiLp.sub_apply]
    exact PiLp.norm_apply_le (x - y) j
  refine le_trans ?_ hcoord
  change dist ((x j : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle) ((y j : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle) ≤ dist (x j) (y j)
  simpa using lipschitzWith_unitAddCircle_coe.dist_le_mul (x j) (y j)

/-! ## Uniqueness of the particle flow -/

/--
**Uniqueness of the particle flow of a Lipschitz velocity on the literal three-torus** (opening
paragraph of §4: "Let `Φ_t : T³ → T³` denote the unique particle flow generated by the velocity
field `u` on `T³` … The existence and uniqueness of this flow for every `t ∈ ℝ` follow from the
global Lipschitz regularity provided in Lemma `lem:global-velocity`").

If `v` is Lipschitz, then `IsParticleFlow v` has at most one inhabitant: the two flows agree as
functions `ℝ → T³ → T³`.

The proof runs on the universal cover.  Fix `t` and `x`; `IsParticleFlow.ode` gives lifted
trajectories `γ, γ'` of the two flows through `x`, both solving the autonomous ODE with the
`ℤ³`-periodic Lipschitz field `v ∘ π`.  Their starting points agree modulo the lattice, so the
translate `γ' + (γ 0 − γ' 0)` is again a solution — the field is unchanged by a translation that
the quotient kills — with the same initial value as `γ`.  Grönwall
(`eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith`) identifies the two curves, and projecting back gives
`Φ_t(x) = Φ'_t(x)`.

Nothing here is needed by `MainLagrangianClauses`: the inverse law `Φ_{−t} ∘ Φ_t = id` is a clause
of its own rather than a consequence of uniqueness.  What this theorem formalises is the definite
article in the paper's "*the* unique particle flow".
-/
theorem IsParticleFlow.unique {K : ℝ≥0} {v : Torus 3 → R3} (hv : LipschitzWith K v)
    {Φ Φ' : ℝ → Torus 3 → Torus 3} (hΦ : IsParticleFlow v Φ) (hΦ' : IsParticleFlow v Φ') :
    Φ = Φ' := by
  have hlift : LipschitzWith K fun z : R3 ↦ v (literalTorusPoint z) := by
    simpa [Function.comp_def] using hv.comp lipschitzWith_literalTorusPoint
  funext t x
  obtain ⟨γ, hγπ, hγ⟩ := hΦ.ode x
  obtain ⟨γ', hγ'π, hγ'⟩ := hΦ'.ode x
  have hstart : literalTorusPoint (γ 0) = literalTorusPoint (γ' 0) := by
    rw [← hγπ 0, ← hγ'π 0, hΦ.initial x, hΦ'.initial x]
  have hshift : literalTorusPoint (γ 0 - γ' 0) = 0 := by
    rw [sub_eq_add_neg, literalTorusPoint_add, literalTorusPoint_neg, hstart, add_neg_cancel]
  have hproj : ∀ s : ℝ, literalTorusPoint (γ' s + (γ 0 - γ' 0)) = literalTorusPoint (γ' s) := by
    intro s
    rw [literalTorusPoint_add, hshift, add_zero]
  have hγode : ∀ s : ℝ, HasDerivAt γ (v (literalTorusPoint (γ s))) s := by
    intro s
    have hderiv := hγ s
    rwa [hγπ s] at hderiv
  have hγ'ode : ∀ s : ℝ, HasDerivAt (fun r : ℝ ↦ γ' r + (γ 0 - γ' 0))
      (v (literalTorusPoint (γ' s + (γ 0 - γ' 0)))) s := by
    intro s
    have hderiv := (hγ' s).add_const (γ 0 - γ' 0)
    rw [hγ'π s] at hderiv
    rwa [hproj s]
  have heq := eq_of_hasDerivAt_of_lipschitzWith hlift hγode hγ'ode (by simp)
  have := congrFun heq t
  rw [hγπ t, hγ'π t, this, hproj t]

/-! ## The particle flow of the constructed velocity is the explicit one -/

namespace ScaleFamily.TorusCellPlacement

open FullVelocityConcrete

variable {p : LocalSeedParameters} {r : ScaleRatios p}
variable (h : PonomarenkoSmoothingWidth)
variable (P : TorusCellPlacement (r := r) (1 + (h : ℝ)))
variable (angularSpeed axialSpeed tau : ℝ)

/-- **The explicit flow is *the* particle flow of the constructed velocity**, at every admissible
parameter tuple with `0 ≤ tau`: any `Φ` satisfying `IsParticleFlow` for that velocity equals
`literalFlow h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau`.

The velocity is Lipschitz by `lipschitzWith_literalVelocity_parameterizedFullGlobalRealTorusSource`
(`FastDynamo/Global/TorusGlobalVelocityLipschitz.lean`, `lem:global-velocity`), so
`IsParticleFlow.unique` applies against the closed-form flow of
`isParticleFlow_literalFlow`. -/
theorem literalFlow_unique (htau : 0 ≤ tau) {Φ : ℝ → Torus 3 → Torus 3}
    (hΦ : IsParticleFlow
      (literalVelocity (parameterizedFullGlobalRealTorusSource h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau))
      Φ) :
    Φ = literalFlow h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau :=
  hΦ.unique
    (lipschitzWith_literalVelocity_parameterizedFullGlobalRealTorusSource h P angularSpeed
      axialSpeed tau htau)
    (isParticleFlow_literalFlow h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau)

/-- **Existence and uniqueness of the particle flow** at the constructed velocity of any
admissible parameter tuple with `0 ≤ tau`, in the shape the paper states in the opening
paragraph of §4: there is exactly one `Φ` with `IsParticleFlow`.  Existence is the closed formula
(`isParticleFlow_literalFlow`), uniqueness is `literalFlow_unique`. -/
theorem existsUnique_isParticleFlow_literalVelocity (htau : 0 ≤ tau) :
    ∃! Φ : ℝ → Torus 3 → Torus 3,
      IsParticleFlow
        (literalVelocity (parameterizedFullGlobalRealTorusSource h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau))
        Φ :=
  ⟨literalFlow h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau,
    isParticleFlow_literalFlow h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau,
    fun _ hΦ ↦ literalFlow_unique h P angularSpeed axialSpeed tau htau hΦ⟩

end ScaleFamily.TorusCellPlacement

/-! ## The particle flow of Theorem 1.2 -/

open HorizontalMode ScaleFamily.TorusCellPlacement

/-- **The particle flow of Theorem 1.2's velocity is unique**: any `Φ` with
`IsParticleFlow mainLiteralVelocity Φ` is the explicit flow at the main parameters — the `Φ`
that `mainTheoremLagrangian_holds` supplies, named `mainLiteralFlow` in
`FastDynamo/Dynamics/MainLagrangianClauses.lean`, where this statement is restated at that name. -/
theorem literalFlow_mainParameterChoice_unique {Φ : ℝ → Torus 3 → Torus 3}
    (hΦ : IsParticleFlow mainLiteralVelocity Φ) :
    Φ = literalFlow globalSeedInterface.smoothingWidth mainParameterChoice.placement
      globalSeedInterface.angularSpeed globalSeedInterface.axialSpeed
      mainParameterChoice.tau :=
  literalFlow_unique globalSeedInterface.smoothingWidth mainParameterChoice.placement
    globalSeedInterface.angularSpeed globalSeedInterface.axialSpeed mainParameterChoice.tau
    mainParameterChoice.tau_nonneg hΦ

/-- **"The unique particle flow generated by the velocity field `u`"** (opening paragraph of §4)
at the velocity of Theorem 1.2: `mainLiteralVelocity` has exactly one particle
flow.  Existence is the closed-form flow, uniqueness is Grönwall on the lifts. -/
theorem existsUnique_isParticleFlow_mainLiteralVelocity :
    ∃! Φ : ℝ → Torus 3 → Torus 3, IsParticleFlow mainLiteralVelocity Φ :=
  existsUnique_isParticleFlow_literalVelocity globalSeedInterface.smoothingWidth
    mainParameterChoice.placement globalSeedInterface.angularSpeed
    globalSeedInterface.axialSpeed mainParameterChoice.tau
    mainParameterChoice.tau_nonneg

end

end FastDynamo
